From f38b6835f8bf6f939c5b5903b7fc3cb1afb74926 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tarpediem <tarpediem@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:59:18 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 25/26] power: supply: max77705: safety monitor review fixes

From an independent review of the first version:
- Switch charging off the way the vendor driver does, CNFG_00 MODE 0x05 ->
  0x04 (buck on, charger off), in addition to CHG_EN = 0, and back on
  with CHG_EN = 1 and MODE 0x05. The monitor starts with the charger off
  (MODE 0x04) and turns it on only after its first check.
- Kick the watchdog only from a read-back safe state: charger off, or on
  with every limit verified. Otherwise force it off and let the watchdog
  run out.
- After a watchdog expiry, force the charger off before checking the
  limits; the kick comes last.
- Keep the float voltage target encoded from DT and compare against it,
  not against the register read back after the write; at probe CV, CC
  and the input limit are written and read back, a mismatch fails the
  probe with the charger off.
- AICL handler: stop at the 100 mA minimum (0 - 1 masked to 0x7F = 3.2 A
  before), bound the loop, take the lock.
- A register read error blocks charging (new BLOCK_IO) and leaves the
  safety timer untouched instead of resetting it; status and charge type
  return read errors instead of using an uninitialised value.
- Unbind: safety_ready is set and checked under the lock, the work only
  re-queues itself while it is set, chgin_work is flushed, so nothing can
  turn charging back on after the disable action.
- WDTEN is re-enabled if found cleared; limit rewrites are counted in the
  log (the USB-C firmware may raise the input limit, see the gts7xl notes).
- charge_behaviour (auto / inhibit-charge) blocks charging through the
  monitor, which also gives a way to test the off switch on hardware.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
